# The Frog and the Man / தவளையும் மனிதனும்
Tamil title: தவளையும் மனிதனும்

This is the story of a frog. Yes, when stories can be written about all manner of things, why should one not write about a frog?

There is a frog in the story of the French Revolution that shook the world. Do you not believe it? I shall tell you everything.

Before the revolution, the French nobles were unable to sleep at night because frogs in the ornamental ponds of their mansions croaked. They therefore issued an order to their farm labourers. All night long, the labourers were to remain awake; whenever the pond frogs cried out, they were to strike and kill them with sticks.

Stories upon stories about the outrageous lives of the French nobility kindled the spark of revolution. Among them was this story about the frog.

By dying, the frog earned the distinction of killing monarchy. Was that an ordinary victory?

Even today, frog races, or rather frog-jumping contests, are held abroad. They even hold frog beauty contests.

This is the story of a frog from our own country, purely indigenous.

“He is an old bandicoot, sir,” people said of Ayyavu, who was called Amavasai. He was exceptionally skilful at hiding and an expert at concealing goods.

Everything among the foreign goods prohibited from import, from razor blades to sovereigns, could be obtained through him. The police circled him, cast nets, and searched his house many times. They found not a trace. However diligently they sniffed around, they could not discover where the goods came from or where he hid them.

It took three years to learn that he was merely an operative in the smuggling trade and that the principal was someone else. Even then, the police could take no action against the principal. He was a prominent figure in the Moolaipattu constituency. He had worked tirelessly for a Congress volunteer who contested an election, secured his victory, and made him an M.L.A. Great Congress leaders had held a special celebration for that victory.

The principal’s “operative” knew every kind of work: slashing, stabbing, arson, and all the rest.

You would not believe that such a man was ultimately reformed by a frog, would you? I too did not believe it at first. Only after he clasped my feet, cried aloud, told me the whole story, and said, “I have reformed now,” did I believe it myself.

I have not said who I am. I am a country physician with special practical knowledge of medicinal leaves. Whether the ailment is itching, cracked skin, a deep ulcer, a cut, a burn, scabies, or a rash, I can cure it with leaf medicine.

One midnight, Amavasai’s wife brought him to me with a bullet wound in his leg that had opened into a deep cavity.

They gave a false village name and false details for everything. If people came secretly at midnight seeking treatment, what other kind of “case” could it be?

The woman said he had received the wound while going to shoot cranes.

I did not ask why they had not taken him immediately to a large hospital or why they had allowed the wound to fester so badly. One fact was already clear to me: this injury had not occurred while he was shooting cranes.

I squeezed the medicinal leaves. I crushed them so hard to extract the juice that even my hand ached. As though asking, “Did you call me for the sake of this sinner?” the juice came only drop by drop. Meanwhile, tears streamed from his wife’s eyes.

“I told him that if he played with fire, it would burn him one day or another. Would he listen?”

From time to time the poor woman sobbed and muttered in this way, as if speaking to herself.

“Why, man? It looks as though someone plastered clay over it and bandaged it.”

“Yes. They said clay was a life-saving remedy that cured every disease.”

“That is true, man. It is life-saving medicine for the poor. But you should first have washed the wound with salt water.”

“Salt water? Physician, I lay in a salt marsh itself for more than an hour.”

Through this exchange, the truth slowly began to emerge.

Only later did I learn the full story.

Goods were customarily brought by sea and handed to him near the salt marsh. Somehow the police had obtained information. They tried to seize him while the contraband was being handed over. They fired a gun, aiming at his head, but the bullet struck his leg. He escaped. The police formed a cordon but could not catch him.

Terrified by the blood spurting from his leg, Amavasai smeared clay upon it. He hid somewhere throughout the day and went to the principal’s house late that night.

He was lodged in a half-collapsed motor shed. The principal did not even find time to come and see him. He was occupied with important work, touring several places with the District Collector to raise donations for building a new pilgrims’ rest house.

After receiving abundant praise from the District Collector and salutations from lower officials, the principal returned to his mansion and was informed of the matter.

Agitated, he went to see Amavasai. The wounded man could barely speak.

The principal did not remain there long. He went to his own room.

“Do you have even a little brain? Why did you allow that fellow to stay here in this condition?”

“He is our man, and he is in a dangerous condition. How could I tell him not to come here? This happened while he was doing our work.”

“Are you arguing with me? What kind of place is this? Do you know who visits here? If people learn that we gave this fellow a place to hide, what will happen to our honour? Did you think of that at all?”

“When I saw his condition, no other thought came to me. Only now that you say it do I understand that allowing him to stay was a mistake.”

“Very well. What medicine did you apply?”

“He could not go to a hospital. Even telling a doctor would arouse suspicion. Last year, when our horse had a sore upon its leg, the veterinary hospital gave us some medicine. A little remained, so we applied it and bandaged him.”

“His wife fell at my feet and wailed: ‘Was my husband not doing the master’s work? He has come to this fate. Could he not be shown to a great doctor? Will you not give me the gift of my marriage pendant by saving his life?’ She said all sorts of things.”

“So I must bring a great doctor, show him this man, and say: ‘He went to bring contraband, was caught by the police, and injured his leg. Cure him; I shall pay, for he was working for me.’ Is that it? Do you think I am a fool? Women always wail in that manner.

“Look here. The man must be removed tonight itself. He must not remain anywhere in this taluk. Do not worry about the expense.”

“If he goes out in this condition, it will be dangerous.”

“Do you not understand that if people learn he is here, it will disgrace us? Give him one or two hundred rupees and tell them to take him to some country physician. Send his wife with him. Tell the man that if he reveals any secret, I shall finish him. The goods are in the well at his house. If he informs the police, they will give him ten years. Make that clear.”

When the accountant conveyed the principal’s anger and arrangements, rage surged within Amavasai. But fear that his life itself was in danger unless he received immediate treatment drove the anger away. They hired a transport cart that very night and came to me.

Amavasai’s wife began with the tale that the injury had occurred while shooting cranes. Then she said it had been an unlucky hour. She lamented for a long time over the unworthy work. Finally, she burst out:

“My husband assumed this danger so that some sinner could earn lakhs upon lakhs. That sinner reduced him to this state and now lives as a great man. Will our fate leave him alone? Will God fail to pay him the proper wages?”

“I shall do everything I can. Had you come four days earlier, I could have given you certainty. Now events will follow whatever fortune your marriage pendant possesses,” I said.

She cried aloud.

“You should have attended to it immediately after the injury. Why did you allow it to fester this far?”

“What could we do, sir? They told us not to go to a hospital or show him to a great doctor.”

“You could at least have brought a good physician to the house.”

“I fell at their feet and begged.”

“Why did they develop such hatred towards you that they told him to seek treatment elsewhere in this condition?”

“Hatred? Towards us? Why should there be hatred? Is it because he used my husband to accumulate profits in the lakhs? No, sir, there is no hatred. There is no hatred. Only a merciless heart, a heart of stone. My husband worked like a dog.”

“Even when a dog they lovingly raise becomes ill, people obtain proper treatment for it.”

“Yes. He regarded us as creatures lower than dogs. He said merely allowing my husband to stay there would disgrace him and drove him out.”

“All right, mother, do not cry. Let him sleep. We shall wait for dawn and see how the medicine I have now applied works.”

After speaking with Amavasai’s wife, I leafed through that day’s newspaper. When I saw one report, I felt both like crying and laughing.

“What is it, sir? Your eyes are wet, yet you are laughing,” the woman asked.

“This is a strange world, mother. The great man for whom your husband laboured to accumulate money, conducted an unworthy trade, and performed degrading work drove him away in this dangerous condition to protect his own safety and status.

“But look at this report in the paper. A frog, see, a frog. Someone has been punished for letting frogs die without showing them compassion.”

“What sort of strange thing is this, sir?” the woman asked.

“Listen, mother,” I said, and read the report aloud:

“An honorary magistrate fined a merchant who exported frogs to foreign countries for food 160 rupees for cruelly treating, or torturing, the frogs. The Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals seized 534 frogs from him. They had been placed in a gunny sack and tied up. [...] The others lay half-dead.

“It was in Bombay that the frog merchant was fined.”

I thought only the woman was listening and that Amavasai was sleeping deeply. His shout revealed that he had been awake.

“Were you not asleep?” I asked.

With anger and tears surging together, he said:

“I was sleeping, sir. I have awakened now. My reason was asleep, and the frog woke me. The frog woke me, sir!

“You read that someone was punished for behaving in a way that caused frogs to die. There is law and justice saying that even a frog must not be tortured, that compassion must be shown and cruelty avoided. That sinner has treated me so cruelly and tortured me. Is there no punishment for this?

“Physician, this did not happen while I was shooting cranes. It was the punishment I received for going to assist the smuggling conducted by that cruel man. I am enduring unbearable pain. It is the proper punishment, sufficient punishment. I deserve it.

“But physician, the sinner for whom I went to perform this work and came to this danger has abandoned me. He told me: ‘Even if you die, do not die where I am. Go somewhere far away.’

“The thought did not arise in that heart: ‘This man has laboured so much and earned the enmity of the village for our sake. He is suffering all this for us, so it is our duty to save him.’

“You read that a man was punished because even a frog must not be tortured. When there is someone to speak on behalf of a frog, will no one show compassion for a human being when they see him tortured?

“I have decided. I shall not remain in hiding. I shall confess my crime. I shall expose the cruel man who used me to become wealthy. There is a judgment that even a frog must not be caught up in cruelty. The law and society will spare me.”

He spoke while struggling for breath.

“Speak slowly, Amavasai. Speak without straining your body,” I said.

“I have reformed, physician. I have reformed! The report about the frog melted me and made me human. I shall never again engage in degrading work. Him, that man...”

As he continued speaking, something happened to Amavasai.

“Oh, my king!” his wife screamed.

I gave him a mouthful of water. After drinking it, he seemed to acquire new strength.

“I shall tell you everything. The principal who reduced me to this state yet lives as a wealthy gentleman, the sinner who lies drinking the cup of pleasure after subjecting me to torment in days when people say even a frog must be shown compassion, his name is...”

His speech began to slur, and his life departed.

The report about the frog had reformed him. He would commit no further wrong. He was gone.

But that “principal” remains.

Amavasai tried to say who he was, but life left him. She knows. Yet the poor woman’s mind was unhinged by his death. She abuses me and everyone she meets. “Must he die so that you may live?” she asks. Maddened, she says all manner of things. How can her speech ever reach a public forum?

Thus “he,” the operative, died, while “He,” the principal, remains. Will He ever lack another “him”?
